Thanks for responding. Some comments inline below.

On 5/29/2012 6:09 PM, Winston Chang wrote:
> Oops, forgot to reply all.
>
>> Does rel() work only with font sizes, or could it (does it) work with any
>> numerical size. I'm thinking in particular margin/padding. Or any unit.
>>
>
> Right now it's only applied for fields named 'size'. It should work for
> units, although in the current implementation, there are no 'size' fields
> that are units.

This idea was part of the margins being in an inheritance relationship.
Then then panel.margin might be equal to 0.75 of the "base" margin while
the plot.margin might be equal to the "base" margin. Then all the
spacings could be scaled up consistently if desired. I don't know if
there is a real use case for this or not. If these get pulled into other
other parts (panel margin into panel.background), then this makes less
sense.

In some ways, this idea parallels the rel_col() discussion in other
parts of this thread.

>> In terms of the inheritance structure:
>> * could legend.text and legend.title both inherit off of something off of
>> text? The obvious name would be legend.text, but that doesn't work here
>> since it is already being used.
>
>> * Can the margins (legend.margin, panel.margin, axis.ticks.margin,
>> plot.margin) inherit off a single margin, and maybe even use rel?
>
> I think this is possible... but is it desirable? It looks like all of the
> margins are set to different values in theme_grey(). In the long term, the
> margins may be merged into other theme elements, which would break this
> inheritance. For example, panel.margin would become panel.background$margin
> or something like that.

(See previous comment)

>> * Should there be some sort of relationship between segment and line? They
>> both have colour, size, and linetype.  I'm thinking segment inherits from
>> line.
>
> I just updated it -- the relationship is that element_segment is gone now.
> :)

Can't argue with that speed of implementation.

>> * There are two align's and a justification (legend.justification,
>> legend.title.align, legend.text.align). I'm not sure what each take and
>> whether they could be related (in an inheritance way) to each other.
>
> I think legend.justification is different from the other two. Maybe it
> could eventually be merged into legend.background...
>
> The legend.title.align and legend.text.align properties are just strings,
> and I think inheritance currently won't work with strings. Also, I think
> they'll eventually go away and be folded into legend.title and legend.text.

That's fine.  I was just going through every possible value of opt and
seeing if any of them fit together in any way that wasn't already expressed.

> * What is the difference between legend.direction and legend.box? Both take
>> "horizontal" or "vertical" as values.
>
> Good question - the ?opts page knows as much as I do.

Didn't think of looking at that page; it's been much improved since the
last time I looked at it (I had been relying on the wiki page).
legend.box talks about multiple guides, so that is probably the relevant
part.

>> * Should the title text really be part of the theme? As I think about the
>> themes, it seems like it shouldn't. If not, then there is the problem of
>> where to put it. It is most similar to the labels of the scales, but it is
>> really not a scale. Short of just making a dedicated function for it, or
>> just putting it into labs(), I'm not sure what to do with it.
>
> I agree, the title should eventually be moved out of the theme. title() is
> already taken, but something like that would be good.

a new argument to labs() would probably work best, although right now
labs() assumes everything it gets is an aes.

>> Is precedence well defined for things like:
>>
>> opts(axis.text = element_text(size=rel(0.5)),
>>      axis.text = element_text(size=4))
>>
>> That is, will it work and will it give a consistent result?
>
> It's not "well-defined", but it works... It just uses the first one. On the
> other hand, if you do this, it uses the second one:
>    opts(axis.text = element_text(size=rel(0.5))) +
>    opts(axis.text = element_text(size=4))

Should these be consistent? Or if not consistent, then at least the
difference should be mentioned in the documentation.

>> Rather than modifying a theme directly as a list, why not utilize the
>> existing opts() infrastructure for assignment? or update?
>
>
> Maybe the + operator could be defined to work this way:
>    mytheme<- theme_grey() + opts(axis.title= ...)

The `+` operator makes much more sense. When writing that, I was
thinking that the syntax I had was clunky, but I couldn't see what was
better. This fits in well with everything else.

>> although the latter would have to either specify everything or fall back to
>> some defaults for unspecified things.
>>
>
> Right, this is still a problem. For top-level items, you still can't just
> change the font family for mytheme$text -- you have to specify everything,
> with element_text(family="", face="", size="", colour="", ...)

Hmm.  Maybe one way around this is by having a "root" class that
everything derives from. That root class could then define defaults for
anything that is not defined further down the hierarchy. This just
shifts the problem to a different element which must have every property
defined. Maybe this root is treated special in assignment to make sure
that it does have everything defined. Or as part of defining a theme,
the root node must have everything given a default, and then any updates
using the element_ functions would only change what they are given
explicitly?

>> How deep does relative sizing work?  For instance:
>>
>> opts(text = element_text(size=16),
>>      axis.text = element_text(size=rel(0.5)),
>>      axis.text.x = element_text(size=rel(0.25)))
>>
>> What would the size of the text on the x axis be?  16*0.5*0.25=2 (rel's
>> are multiplicative up the tree)? 16*0.5=8 (only the first rel going down
>> the tree matters and any subsequent ones are ignored)? 16*0.25=4 (only the
>> last rel in the tree matters, and it is in reference to the last absolute
>> value)?  I think the first one is what it should be.
>>
>
> At each level of inheritance, it multiplies, so it would be 16*0.5*0.25=2.

Excellent!

> In general, I really like the new design. I think it fixes many problems of
>> the old one, and I don't see any glaringly obvious problems with it.
>
>
> Great, thanks for the feedback!

Thanks for the work! Sitting back and saying "what about...?" is easier
than actually writing it :)

